From 0c7e3da6efb042db905c6ed79b17865aafeba5b5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 06:20:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Never lose a rendered video to an optional finishing pass
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Job #51878 rendered its base video, stored it, synced some scenes, then
hit a provider refusal on one scene and threw the whole job away. The user
got nothing — no video, and no sign that the part they actually paid the
most for had succeeded.

The pass uploads the completed base a few lines before the loop, with a
comment saying a finishing failure can therefore be surfaced without
regenerating scenes. It just never did it: `generateLipSyncWithReplicate`
was called with no try/catch, so one refusal unwound the entire function.
The character engine's own pass has failed soft since it shipped, for
exactly this reason. This one didn't, and the inconsistency is the bug.

A refused scene now keeps its base footage, is recorded as `skipped` with
the provider's reason, and the pass carries on. This is an optional
finishing stage over a video that already exists and is already paid for;
one refusal should cost that scene its sync and nothing else.

It cannot mask billed-but-lost work. That case is the `provider_succeeded`
checkpoint, which still throws, because a receipt exists for it — the
distinction the checkpoint states were built to make.

Silence would be its own bug, so VideoJob gains `skippedSceneCount`.
Without it someone watches a finished video, sees one character's mouth
not moving, and has no way to learn the provider refused that scene. A
skipped scene never carries a provider event, since the event is the
billing receipt, and there is a test pinning that.

This does not explain why LatentSync refuses that particular segment
twice across two different storyboards — a repeat on the same scene is not
transient, whatever the recorded classification says. It stops that
refusal from costing the whole video while the cause is found.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Fm1CGTe9kKKt48ZPVyQMh1
